'''Catholicism''' is a [[Christianity|Christian]] tradition comprising those churches that recognise the authority of the [[Pope]] as head of the universal church and maintain an {{W|Episcopal polity|episcopal}} hierarchy of bishops, priests, and deacons. Catholic teaching affirms the {{W|Trinity}}, the seven {{W|sacraments}}, and the apostolic succession of the episcopate, and is distinguished from other Christian traditions by its insistence on papal primacy and its body of [[canon law]] administered through ecclesiastical courts. Catholicism is the predominant Christian tradition of [[Illypnia]] and the largest single Christian communion in [[Kaftia]] and much of [[Lurandia]].

Since the [[Great Catholic Schism]] of 1313, Catholicism has been divided between two communions, each headed by its own pope: the [[Petran Catholic Church|Petran Catholic]] communion, whose pope reigns from the [[Papal States]], a sovereign state adjacent to [[Volisania]]; and the [[Zamarran Catholic]] communion, whose pope is seated at [[Zamarra]] in [[Kingdom of Kastruneto|Kastruneto]]. Each communion maintains that its pope is the legitimate head of the universal Catholic Church, and the division remains unresolved into the present day. The [[Burawi Catholic Church]] of [[Kaftia]], founded in 573, is in full communion with the Petran hierarchy while governing its internal affairs autonomously under the [[Cardinal of Burawa]].

== Terminology ==

The term ''Catholicism'' derives from the {{W|Greek language|Greek}} ''katholikos'', meaning "universal", and designates the tradition of churches that understand themselves as the universal church in continuous succession from the [[Aetolians|Aetolian]] Christian community of the early centuries. Adherents are termed ''Catholics''. Following the Great Catholic Schism, the labels ''Petran'' and ''Zamarran'' came to distinguish the two communions by the location of their respective papal courts: the Petran communion by way of the [[Papal States]] and the Zamarran communion by way of [[Zamarra]]. The [[Burawi Catholic Church|Burawi Catholic]] tradition of Kaftia is described as Catholic by virtue of its communion with the Petran hierarchy, though it maintains a distinct liturgical rite and an autonomous internal government.

== History ==

=== Early church and the rise of the papacy ===

Christianity spread through [[Illypnia]] in the first centuries of the era, and ecclesiastical authority became progressively concentrated in the papal states around [[Volisania]]. During the Middle Ages, the Catholic Church dominated the mainland's cultural life: through monasteries and cathedral schools, the Church was responsible for education across much of Illypnia, and the papacy reached the height of its power during the High Middle Ages. In 1095 the Pope called a crusade against non-Christian powers holding territories regarded as Christian, and the [[Sartan Inquisition|Sartan]] and [[Kastrunetian Inquisition]]s began their operations, persecuting [[Magdalenan Church|Magdalenians]] as well despite their Christian profession.

The Church suffered a serious blow in the mid-14th century from the [[Red Death]], one of the most deadly pandemics in recorded history, which killed an estimated 350 million people in Illypnia alone. The pandemic was followed by increased persecution of Jews, Magdalenians, pagans, Muslims, and foreigners across the Illypnian kingdoms, and more than one hundred plague epidemics recurred over subsequent generations. The Renaissance of the 14th to 16th centuries unfolded in part under the joint patronage of royalty, the nobility, the Catholic churches, and an emerging merchant class, with patrons in [[Echia]], Volisania, and the Papal States funding prolific artists.

=== Christianisation of Kaftia ===

{{main|Christianisation of Burawa}}

Christianity reached the Kaftian coast through [[Volisanians|Volisanian]] merchants and monks in the early 6th century, during the reign of [[Mahad I of Burawa|Mahad I]] (r. 538–559). Volisanian traders had maintained commercial networks with the port cities of [[Mazar]] and [[Iskhal]] since the [[Mesogean League]] period, and Volisanian monks established the first churches in the harbour districts of both cities. The new faith spread initially among the urban merchant class.

The conversion of [[Iacob I of Burawa|Iacob I]] (r. 573–612) elevated Christianity from a merchant community to a royal religion. Iacob I established the [[Burawi Catholic Church]] under royal patronage in 573, modelling its hierarchy on the Volisanian Catholic structure and placing it in communion with the broader Catholic communion while granting it internal autonomy under a Burawi episcopal hierarchy. The Christianisation of the Kaftian interior proceeded over the following centuries: Waaq worship was suppressed in the highlands after the [[Northern Conquest of Burawa|Northern Conquest]] (893–925), and the [[Ndogalu]] tradition of the northern basin was suppressed during the Imperial era through missionary activity and the construction of churches at former Ndogalu centres of worship.

=== Great Catholic Schism ===

{{main|Great Catholic Schism}}

By the early 14th century, ecclesiastical authority in much of Illypnia was concentrated in the papal states around Volisania, and several kingdoms to the south and east regarded papal taxation and jurisdiction as burdensome. Disputes over church authority, taxation, indulgences, purgatory, and Curial administration came to a head after the death of the sitting pope. The conclave elected the Kastrunetean [[Augustin V]], who refused to relocate the papal court to the papal states and instead established his court at [[Zamarra]] on [[Lake Zamarra]]. The decision created a permanent rival centre of Catholic authority and formalised the schism in 1313.

The Zamarran court was recognised by [[Kingdom of Kastruneto|Kastruneto]], [[Kingdom of Breisland|Breisland]], and [[Kingdom of Nabia|Nabia]], while the papal states retained the support of [[Volisania]], [[Sarta]], and [[Kingdom of Jacan|Jacan]]. The division reflected doctrinal disputes as well as fiscal systems, trade interests, and diplomatic alignments. The [[Jade Sea War]] in eastern Fosia had reduced revenue from the southern trade routes linking Illypnia to the wider region, compounding the economic grievances of the landlocked southern kingdoms.

=== Two Centuries War ===

{{main|Two Centuries War}}

The schism escalated into the [[Two Centuries War]], which began in 1337. Fought between the Petran Catholic states and the Zamarran Catholic states, the conflict consisted of frontier offensives, raids, truces, and regional campaigns rather than a single continuous war, with the most sustained fighting in the valleys of the [[Rio Grande]] and [[Rio Verde]], especially in the border zone between Sarta and Kastruneto. The war devastated much of central Illypnia and became one of the most important conflicts of the Middle Ages, marking both the height of chivalric warfare and its subsequent decline.

The end of the war did not heal the division. Petran and Zamarran alignments continued to shape court politics, university teaching, and church administration across Illypnia, and the two papacies continued to claim rulership over the universal church. In Sarta, the schism reinforced existing north-south divisions: the north remained more closely tied to Petran Catholic authority, while the south maintained stronger ecclesiastical and trade connections to Jacan and the Zamarran sphere.

=== Colonial expansion ===

From the 16th century, Catholicism spread to [[Abyala]] and [[Lurandia]] through the colonial ventures of the Catholic powers. The [[Burawi Catholic Church]] endorsed maritime expansion as a means of extending Christianity, and the establishment of parishes in [[Lacashe]] was among the first acts of the Burawi colonial administration in 1516. In the Sartan colonial sphere, Petran Catholic clergy took over the formal religious life of [[Guajicutea]] after the expulsion of earlier missions tied to Burawa and Volisania, and the 1554 settlement following the First Guaji Civil War gave Petran Catholicism a firm foothold across the south and west of the island. The Volisanian colonial territories of [[Balboa]] and [[Balisca]] received Catholic institutions from the earliest period of settlement, and the [[Archdiocese of Sant Bart]] became the principal Catholic see of the Balboan isthmus.

Catholic colonial authorities also persecuted heterodox Christian movements in the New World. Cathar settlements in the Volisanian colonies were systematically targeted in the 16th and 17th centuries, most intensively during the [[Reapers' War]], which was framed in part as a holy crusade to eliminate heretical Christian movements; Cathar missions were destroyed and large numbers of indigenous allies enslaved. This campaign left a lasting legacy of bitterness toward Catholic institutions in the affected territories.

=== The Lacashe schism ===

{{main|Lacashe Church}}

When [[Lacashe]] declared independence from Burawa in 1789 rather than accept the abolition of slavery, the colonial bishopric faced a crisis. Bishop [[Filipos of Lacashe|Filipos]] aligned with the planter government and declared the establishment of an independent [[Lacashe Church]], with himself as its head. The Burawi Catholic Church excommunicated Filipos and declared the Lacashe Church schismatic. The division was primarily political rather than theological, and the Lacashe Church maintained doctrines and liturgical practices largely identical to those of the Burawi Church. The schism persisted after the [[War of Liberation (1853–1856)|War of Liberation]] and was resolved only in the early 1890s, when the Lacashe Church was formally reincorporated into the Burawi Catholic Church as the Diocese of Lacashe, with its bishops restored to communion with the Cardinal of Burawa and through him with the Petran hierarchy.

=== The twentieth century ===

The twentieth century brought the sharpest reversals of Catholic institutional power since the Great Catholic Schism. In [[Balisca]], the revolutionary period of 1918–1921 produced a wave of anti-clericalism and iconoclasm directed especially at the Catholic Church, whose colonial-era repression of Cathars and other non-Catholic groups was invoked in revolutionary propaganda. In May 1919, attacks destroyed dozens of churches, friaries, convents, and schools across Balisca, communes expropriated Church properties, and Catholic armed militias — the ''Guerrilleros de Cristo Rey'' — formed in response before their dissolution in 1930.

In Sarta, the National Party dictatorship of the interwar period targeted [[Zamarran Catholic]] communities from the 1930s, reading their religious ties to Kastruneto and the wider Zamarran world as a political threat, and internment of minorities and political opponents accompanied the repression. During the [[Great Adonian War]] (1935–1947), the Sartan regime invaded Kastruneto in 1940 with the declared ambition of eliminating the Zamarran Church, drawing western Kastruneto into the wider conflict. The war's end and the subsequent tribunals and reconciliation began a slow normalisation of relations between the communions, though the schism itself remained unresolved.

== Branches ==

=== Petran Catholic Church ===

The [[Petran Catholic Church|Petran Catholic]] communion is the larger of the two communions and is headed by the Pope, who reigns from the [[Papal States]], a sovereign state adjacent to [[Volisania]]. The Petran communion comprises the Catholic churches of the historic papal alignment: in Illypnia, the churches of Volisania, Sarta, and Jacan; in Lurandia, the Catholic communities of [[Balboa]], [[Balisca]], [[Guajicutea]], and [[Potocsí]]; and, in communion, the [[Burawi Catholic Church]] of Kaftia and the Catholic Sabian community of [[Sabia]], whose tradition derives from the Sartan homeland of the Sabin settlers. Petran Catholicism is the dominant religion of Potocsí (71.2 per cent of the population) and Guajicutea (66 per cent), the largest single denomination in Balboa (approximately 40 per cent), and a major minority community in Balisca (10 per cent). The [[Burawi Catholic Church]] recognises the spiritual authority of the Petran pope while governing its internal affairs autonomously.

=== Zamarran Catholic ===

The [[Zamarran Catholic]] communion is headed by the pope seated at [[Zamarra]] on [[Lake Zamarra]] in [[Kingdom of Kastruneto|Kastruneto]], and comprises the churches of the kingdoms that recognised the Zamarran court at the Great Catholic Schism: Kastruneto, [[Kingdom of Breisland|Breisland]], and [[Kingdom of Nabia|Nabia]]. The Zamarran communion maintains the same sacramental and episcopal structure as the Petran church, and the division between the two communions is regarded by most historians as having originated in disputes over church governance, taxation, and jurisdiction rather than in fundamental doctrinal divergence. Zamarran communities also exist as minorities within Petran-aligned states, most notably in southern Sarta, where they were subjected to persecution in the 1930s and 1940s.

=== Burawi Catholic Church ===

{{main|Burawi Catholic Church}}

The [[Burawi Catholic Church]] is the principal Catholic communion of [[Southwestern Kaftia]]. Founded in 573 by [[Iacob I of Burawa|Iacob I]], the Church is in full communion with the [[Petran Catholic Church]] and recognises the spiritual authority of the Pope, but governs its internal affairs — liturgy, ecclesiastical appointments, canon law, and diocesan organisation — autonomously under the leadership of the [[Cardinal of Burawa]], whose seat has been at [[Weli Markos]] since 1709. The Church conducts its liturgy in the [[Burawi language]] according to the Burawi rite, a distinctive tradition developed from Volisanian antecedents, and has sponsored monastic orders including the [[Order of Saint Guled]], the [[Bartay Society]], the [[Order of Saint Iacob]], and the [[Order of the Holy Apostles]]. With approximately 115 million adherents, it is the largest single Christian communion across the former Burawi sphere.

=== Sabian Christianity ===

{{main|Sabins}}

The [[Sabins|Sabin]] communities of [[Sabia]] are descended from settlers who emigrated from [[Sarta]] in the 5th century, arriving by approximately 438 as part of the same wave of [[Mesogean]] Christian contact that reached the Burawi coast. They brought with them the Christian faith and established their own ecclesiastical structures rather than falling under the [[Burawi Catholic Church]]. The Sabin Christian population is divided between a Catholic community and a non-Catholic community. The Catholic Sabians are [[Petran Catholic Church|Petran Catholics]], the tradition of their Sartan homeland, and maintain an episcopal hierarchy distinct from the Burawi Catholic Church; they constitute approximately 18 per cent of the population of Sabia. The non-Catholic Christian Sabians belong to a separate Christian tradition that is not in communion with the Catholic churches.

=== Former branches ===

The [[Lacashe Church]] existed as an independent Catholic communion from its establishment by Bishop Filipos in 1789 until its reincorporation into the Burawi Catholic Church as the Diocese of Lacashe in the early 1890s. The division was political in origin, arising from the Lacashe planter government's refusal to accept the metropolitan abolition of slavery, and its resolution followed the collapse of the slaveholding order after the War of Liberation.

== Theology and practice ==

Catholicism is a {{W|Trinitarianism|trinitarian}} tradition, affirming the Father, the Son, and the Holy Spirit as three persons of one God. The Catholic churches administer the seven {{W|sacraments}} of baptism, confirmation, eucharist, reconciliation, marriage, holy orders, and anointing of the sick, and maintain {{W|apostolic succession}} through the episcopate. Polity is episcopal, with the Church organised into dioceses headed by bishops, and canon law administered through ecclesiastical courts with historical jurisdiction over marriage, inheritance, and moral discipline.

Liturgy is conducted according to distinct rites. The Petran rite prevails across the Petran communion, while the Burawi rite, conducted in the [[Burawi language]] and incorporating indigenous Kaftian musical and poetic forms, constitutes a distinctive tradition within the broader communion. The veneration of saints is practised across the Catholic churches, with each communion and church maintaining its own sanctoral calendar alongside the shared tradition.

Catholicism is distinguished from other Christian traditions of Adonia by its affirmation of papal primacy. The [[Lathrianism|Lathrian]] movement of Kaftia is explicitly {{W|Nontrinitarianism|nontrinitarian}}, rejecting the doctrine of the Trinity that the Catholic churches affirm. [[Catharism]] is a dualist tradition whose beliefs the Catholic Church has denounced since its emergence, and which Catholic authorities persecuted both in Illypnia and in the colonies. The [[Sabins|Sabin]] Christians of [[Sabia]] are divided between a Catholic community, which follows the [[Petran Catholic Church|Petran Catholic]] tradition of its Sartan origins under an episcopal hierarchy separate from the Burawi Catholic Church, and a non-Catholic Christian community outside the Catholic communion. The [[Magdalenan Church|Magdalenian]] communities of Illypnia were persecuted by the Inquisitions despite their Christian profession.

== Distribution ==

=== Illypnia ===

The Petran communion prevails in the historic papal-aligned states of [[Volisania]], [[Sarta]], and [[Kingdom of Jacan|Jacan]], and in [[Potocsí]], where Petran Catholics constitute 71.2 per cent of the population. The Zamarran communion prevails in [[Kingdom of Kastruneto|Kastruneto]], [[Kingdom of Breisland|Breisland]], and [[Kingdom of Nabia|Nabia]], with Zamarran minority communities in southern Sarta and elsewhere. The [[Papal States]] constitute the territorial core of the Petran communion.

